# MCSP Assembly / Disassembly Official Architecture 0.37

Status: Constructive architecture amendment
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Supersedes/extends: ASSEMBLY-DOMINANCE-0.27

## Official disciplines

MCSP formally recognizes two reciprocal machine-realization disciplines:

    ASSEMBLY
    DISASSEMBLY

Both are first-class project disciplines for implementation, qualification, reconstruction, inspection and recovery.

## Assembly

ASSEMBLY projects a qualified MCSP semantic/scaffold obligation toward a machine realization.

    MCSP semantic obligation
    -> qualified scaffold
    -> assembly realization
    -> encoding
    -> machine behavior

Assembler syntax, mnemonic spelling, register names, directives, object format and concrete instruction sequence are realization-specific and SHALL NOT define MCSP meaning.

Independent assemblers/ISAs are encouraged as preservation witnesses.

## Disassembly

DISASSEMBLY inspects/reconstructs machine realization evidence toward an MCSP-qualified projection.

    machine encoding/artifact
    -> disassembly/structural inspection
    -> realization observations
    -> MCSP projection
    -> qualification comparison

Disassembly SHALL NOT be treated as an automatic inverse that recovers original source, documentary names, comments, assembler directives, provenance or semantic intent.

A disassembler reports a realization interpretation. MCSP qualification determines which observed distinctions are materially relevant.

## Reciprocity

Assembly and disassembly are reciprocal qualification directions, not mathematical inverses:

    ASSEMBLE(S,H) -> R
    DISASSEMBLE(R,H) -> O
    PROJECT(O,scope) -> S'

Qualification asks whether S' PRESERVES the applicable pre-settled material invariant of S.

Source-text equality is not required.
Instruction-sequence equality is not required.
Binary equality across materially different realizations is not required.
Semantic preservation under declared applicability is required.

## Official project rule

From 0.37 onward, material assembly witnesses SHOULD retain proportional disassembly evidence when it improves reconstruction, audit or qualification.

Disassembly evidence SHOULD include where applicable:
- artifact hash;
- architecture/object format;
- section/symbol boundary relevant to the test;
- decoded instruction evidence;
- realization-specific addresses/encodings when material to reconstruction;
- explicit distinction between observation and semantic conclusion.

## Recovery

Qualified release checkpoints SHOULD retain enough source/build/disassembly evidence for deterministic reconstruction or contradiction analysis.

## Security

Disassembly does not grant authority to inspect artifacts outside user-authorized project scope.

## C boundary

C remains a temporary testing stud only. Assembly and disassembly are the official primary machine-level implementation/inspection disciplines.

Kernel remains DETERMINATE CONDITION.
